{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that the preference is defensible 'only if' rationality is defined by rule-adherence rather than probable truth presents a false dichotomy that ignores Popper's concept of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness). Popper explicitly argued that while we cannot assign probability to theories, we can rationally prefer the better-corroborated theory because it has withstood tests that falsified its competitors, suggesting it is closer to the truth (higher verisimilitude), not merely because it obeys a methodological rule. By asserting that the *only* defense is a radical separation of rationality from expectation (acting without expectation of success), the target misrepresents Popper's actual solution: the scientist acts on the best-corroborated theory precisely *because* they expect it to be a better guide to truth than falsified alternatives, even if this expectation is not probabilistic. The target's 'cost' (acting without expectation) is therefore a straw man; the real cost of Popper's view is accepting a non-probabilistic, fallibilist notion of truth-likeness, which the target fails to address.","premise":"Popper introduced 'verisimilitude' to explain why scientists prefer better-corroborated theories without relying on induction or probability, arguing such theories are logically preferred as being closer to the truth.","successor_question":"How does the target's assertion that rational action requires 'no expectation of success' align with Popper's explicit statements that we choose theories because we believe them to be better approximations of the truth?","target":"d32d72987f32122368583d8ec4e5d9c332a7160bcf21e0195c546bc71898d70a"}]}